1. Clinical neurophysiology for brain–body interfacing
Interfacing the brain with the body is a central challenge in contemporary clinical neurophysiology and neuroengineering. Neurological injuries such as spinal cord injury (SCI), stroke, and limb loss disrupt the bidirectional flow of information between the nervous system and the periphery, leading to impairments in voluntary movement, sensation, and body perception(Courtine and Sofroniew, 2019, Krakauer et al., 2012, Langhorne et al., 2011, Makin and Flor, 2020).
Clinical neurophysiology provides the quantitative and methodological foundation for brain–body interfacing by linking nervous system signals to functional output across multiple levels of organization (Farina et al., 2014, Nicolas-Alonso and Gomez-Gil, 2012). Traditional clinical neurophysiology has focused on the assessment of neural function using electrophysiological techniques such as electroencephalogram (EEG), electromyography (EMG), nerve conduction studies, and evoked potentials, primarily for diagnostic purposes (Cruccu et al., 2008, Preston and Shapiro, 2012, Rossini et al., 2015). However, the same tools and principles now underpin therapeutic and assistive neurotechnologies that actively interact with neural circuits. Within this framework, interfacing refers explicitly to both “reading” and “writing” neural information. Decoding encompasses the extraction of meaningful variables (e.g., movement intent) from neural or myoelectric signals recorded at central or peripheral levels (Nicolelis, 2001). Actuation refers to the delivery of targeted outputs, via electrical stimulation or robotic assistance, that modify neural activity or directly generate movement and sensation (Grill et al., 2009, Wolpaw, 2013). This bidirectional definition distinguishes modern brain–body interfaces from earlier assistive technologies that relied solely on mechanical substitution or passive support (Coscia et al., 2019, Graczyk et al., 2024, Raspopovic, 2020).
Brain–body interfaces aim to restore this flow by establishing artificial communication pathways that link neural activity to bodily function (de Vos and Meier, 2024, Thibaut and Martens, 2024). Crucially, this interaction is inherently bidirectional: neural signals must be decoded to infer intention, while appropriate information must be written back to the nervous system or peripheral effectors to generate action, sensation, or physiological regulation(Lebedev and Nicolelis, 2006, Losanno et al., 2023, Micera et al., 2020, Raspopovic, 2021). This bidirectional perspective has driven a shift from early open-loop systems toward closed-loop architectures that integrate neural decoding with stimulation-based feedback, reflecting the fundamental role of sensory, proprioceptive, and interoceptive signals in shaping motor control, embodiment, and activity-dependent plasticity (Dawson et al., 2024).
Despite the advances of invasive neural interfaces (Badi et al., 2021, Musk and Neuralink, , 2019, Rowald et al., 2022), most clinical and experimental systems remain predominantly decoding-oriented. However, users equipped with these systems must substitute missing afferent information with vision, increasing cognitive demand and degrading precision in fine manipulation (Ghez et al., 1995, Johansson and Flanagan, 2009, Wolpert et al., 1995). Closing the loop is therefore the defining requirement for naturalistic neural control. In intact physiology, efferent commands are continuously exploited to modulate by afferent feedback both at the central level and in reflexes (Scott, 2004, Shadmehr et al., 2010). When afferent pathways are lost there are downstream consequences that extend beyond control down to body representation and maladaptive plasticity, including phantom phenomena and chronic pain (Flor et al., 2006, Lotze and Moseley, 2007, Risso et al., 2022). Restoring feedback reintroduces the physiological signal that gates activity-dependent plasticity in cortical and spinal circuits, enabling durable recovery (Abbott and Nelson, 2000, Jackson et al., 2006). Accordingly, recent work emphasizes that artificially evoked afferent input can be leveraged therapeutically to guide adaptive reorganization after injury, rather than merely compensating for lost function (Cimolato and Raspopovic, 2024, Courtine and Sofroniew, 2019).
The aim of this chapter is to provide an integrated overview of decoding and actuation technologies in clinical neurophysiology, and to examine how these components are combined into closed-loop brain–body interfaces across multiple neuroanatomical levels. We further discuss emerging methodological frameworks, including computational modeling and artificial intelligence, that have been increasingly adopted in recent years to enable adaptive, personalized closed-loop systems and to accelerate clinical translation.
2. Decoding technologies: How we “read” the nervous system
This section reviews how neural information is extracted from the nervous system to infer user intent or status, focusing on decoding approaches across multiple neuroanatomical levels (Fig. 1). We emphasize decoding-only systems that transform neural activity into control variables for communication, prosthetic control, or virtual interaction, without direct sensory or motor actuation.
