2.1. Intracortical decoding
Intracortical brain–computer interfaces (BCI) decode movement- or action-related variables directly from spiking activity recorded using microelectrode arrays implanted in cortical gray matter, most commonly within the primary motor or premotor cortex (Lebedev and Nicolelis, 2006), providing access to high temporal and spatial resolution neural signals.
Pioneering clinical studies from the BrainGate consortium established that intended arm and hand movements can be reliably decoded from primary motor cortex (M1) activity in individuals with chronic tetraplegia. Using 96-channel Utah microelectrode arrays and Kalman filter–based decoders, Hochberg et al. (2012) demonstrated continuous three-dimensional reach and grasp control of a robotic arm, enabling functional actions such as object manipulation. This work provided the first proof that high-dimensional motor intent remains decodable years after paralysis (Hochberg et al., 2012). Subsequent studies substantially expanded decoding dimensionality and performance. Collinger et al. (2013) showed that signals from two intracortical arrays implanted in M1 could be decoded into seven degrees of freedom. This in particular supported the possibility to coordinate control of 3D translation, orientation, and grasp aperture with success rates exceeding 90% on complex tasks (Collinger et al., 2013). Follow-up work further increased control dimensionality to ten degrees of freedom by incorporating wrist and hand kinematics demonstrating scalable decoding of complex arm movements (Wodlinger et al., 2014). Beyond basic kinematic decoding, later studies investigated how task context and object interaction modulate cortical representations. Downey et al. (2017) showed that many M1 units exhibit context-dependent tuning, with increased firing during object-directed reaches compared to matched movements in free space. Ignoring this modulation degraded decoding performance, highlighting the importance of incorporating task context into decoder design (Downey et al., 2017). Related work demonstrated that grasp force and effort are also represented in M1 and can be decoded as discrete or graded variables, providing a neural substrate for force-aware prosthetic control (Rastogi et al., 2020).
Building on early demonstrations of gross arm and hand decoding, recent work has substantially expanded the speed, dexterity, and functional scope of intracortical motor decoding. Advances in high-density recordings coupled with modern machine-learning approaches have enabled fine-grained extraction of complex motor representations beyond simple cursor or whole-arm control. An example is the handwriting BCI developed by Willett et al. (2021), in which Utah arrays implanted in premotor cortex were combined with a recurrent neural network decoder to translate attempted handwriting movements into text in real time. This system achieved typing speeds of approximately 90 characters per minute with ∼ 94% raw accuracy (exceeding 99% with autocorrection), approaching typical smartphone typing performance (Willett et al., 2021). Extending this paradigm to dexterous motor control, Willsey et al. (2025) demonstrated continuous decoding up to four simultaneous degrees of freedom in a person with tetraplegia. This enabled complex interactions which could be exploited for virtual object interactions (Willsey et al., 2025). Notably, participants reported increased engagement, recreation, and social connectedness, underscoring how high-resolution intracortical decoding can support not only functional control but also meaningful real-world use.
While most intracortical decoding studies target the hand and arm regions of M1, concurrent works suggest that signals from sulcal cortex, white matter, and association areas may offer additional discriminability for finger-level decoding (Bouton et al., 2021). Advances in high-density recordings and machine learning have further improved decoding robustness and speed, and animal studies using ultra-high-channel-count probes such as Neuropixels point toward future scalability (Steinmetz et al., 2021). Some groups have also explored signals from posterior parietal cortex to decode goal-directed movement intentions, highlighting the contribution of association cortices to higher-level aspects of motor planning (Haghi et al., 2024).
2.2. Electrocorticography decoding
Electrocorticography (ECoG) provides a window into cortical population dynamics, occupying a unique position between non-invasive EEG and penetrating intracortical recordings. By placing electrodes directly on the cortical surface (either subdurally or epidurally) ECoG achieves higher signal-to-noise ratio, broader bandwidth, and superior spatial specificity compared to scalp EEG, while avoiding the tissue penetration and long-term signal degradation often associated with microelectrode arrays (Slutzky, 2019, Wu et al., 2024).
Early proof-of-concept studies established that volitional motor intent can be decoded from ECoG in real time. Leuthardt and colleagues first demonstrated online one-dimensional cursor control using spectral features extracted from sensorimotor cortex, showing rapid learning and high accuracy after minimal training (Leuthardt et al., 2004). Shortly thereafter, Schalk et al. extended this work to continuous decoding of two-dimensional cursor trajectories, revealing that both spectral power and local motor potential (LMP) encode movement direction and position with performance comparable to that achieved using intracortical recordings (Schalk et al., 2008). Subsequent work expanded ECoG decoding beyond simple cursor paradigms to continuous reconstruction of limb kinematics. Using linear models and state-space formulations, including linear state-space implementations, multiple studies demonstrated decoding of hand and arm position, velocity, and speed in two- and three-dimensional space during naturalistic reaching movements (Bundy et al., 2018, Pistohl et al., 2008, Wang et al., 2012). Notably, informative signals were found not only in M1, but also in premotor, parietal, and even prefrontal regions, supporting a distributed representation of motor control (Bundy et al., 2018, Schalk et al., 2008). ECoG has also proven effective for decoding kinetic variables. Continuous reconstruction of isometric grip force has been demonstrated with high fidelity, with high-gamma activity and LMP emerging as dominant predictors (Flint et al., 2014).
A major advantage of ECoG lies in its ability to resolve fine somatotopic organization on the cortical surface, enabling decoding of individual finger movements and complex hand gestures (Branco et al., 2017, Korik et al., 2018). Multiple studies have demonstrated reliable classification of finger flexion/extension and multi-digit gestures using high-density ECoG grids placed over sensorimotor cortex (Kubánek et al., 2009, Miller et al., 2009). Beyond discrete classification, continuous reconstruction of finger trajectories has also been achieved, with correlation coefficients approaching those observed in intracortical systems (Acharya et al., 2010, Hotson et al., 2016).
A distinct translational trajectory of ECoG-based motor decoding has been pursued by the Clinatec research center in Grenoble. Using a fully implanted, wireless epidural ECoG system (WIMAGINE), two 64-channel electrode arrays were placed bilaterally over sensorimotor cortex in participants with chronic tetraplegia(Mestais et al., 2015). Neural activity associated with motor imagery was decoded in real time to control both a virtual avatar and a full-body exoskeleton. Over a 24-month protocol, the participant achieved voluntary control of up to eight degrees of freedom, including bilateral upper-limb movements and lower-limb stepping commands (Benabid et al., 2019, Larzabal et al., 2021). Decoder stability was maintained for several weeks without retraining, highlighting the feasibility of chronic, wireless ECoG for complex motor assistive technologies.
Except for movement intention, ECoG has emerged as one of the most powerful modalities for decoding speech-related neural activity. High-density ECoG recordings over ventral sensorimotor cortex reveal detailed articulatory representations that can be mapped to words and continuous speech (Anumanchipalli et al., 2019, Mugler et al., 2014). Complementary studies have shown that ECoG signals recorded from superior temporal gyrus can be used to reconstruct perceived and imagined speech, extending decoding beyond motor production to auditory and linguistic representations (Akbari et al., 2019, Pasley et al., 2012).
2.3. Electroencephalography decoding
EEG is the most widely deployed non-invasive neural recording modality in neurophysiology, because it combines millisecond-scale temporal resolution with low cost, portability, and an excellent safety profile (Lotte et al., 2018, Nicolas-Alonso and Gomez-Gil, 2012). EEG signals measured at the scalp primarily reflect synchronized postsynaptic potentials in large neuronal populations, but the same biophysics that makes EEG non-invasive (volume conduction through cerebrospinal fluid, skull, and scalp) also reduces spatial specificity and attenuates high-frequency components compared with invasive recordings (Buzsáki et al., 2012, Slutzky, 2019). In practice, EEG must contend with low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), strong non-neural contamination (ocular, facial, and neck muscle artifacts), and nonstationarity across sessions and contexts (Lotte et al., 2018).
In visual EEG paradigms, particularly those based on repetitive or flickering stimuli, sustained use can lead to visual fatigue and reduced user comfort, limiting practical deployment (Cao et al., 2014, Xie et al., 2016). Current research therefore focuses on reducing visual fatigue and improving usability through alternative stimulation strategies, including code-modulated paradigms based on pseudorandom sequences, and hybrid paradigms that integrate multiple neural responses (e.g., SSVEP and P300) to enhance robustness and user performance. In parallel, advancements in EEG hardware are moving toward dry electrodes and wearable acquisition systems, aiming to reduce setup time and improve user comfort. One of the major branches of EEG decoding focuses on reliable communication via evoked potentials. P300 spellers use brain responses linked to attention and have long provided a reliable way for people with severe paralysis to communicate (e.g., writing text), especially when stimulus timing is optimized and language models are used to support text selection (Farwell and Donchin, 1988, Krusienski et al., 2008, Mak and Wolpaw, 2009). Faster communication is possible when event-evoked potentials are combined with steady-state visual evoked potentials (SSVEP), where users focus on flickering visual targets and frequency-specific brain responses can be decoded efficiently (Chen et al., 2015, Muller-Putz and Pfurtscheller, 2008, Nakanishi et al., 2018). More recently, hybrid paradigms have been proposed that combine SSVEP and P300 responses, leveraging complementary neural mechanisms to improve classification accuracy and robustness (Li et al., 2013, Xu et al., 2013).
Another foundational branch of EEG BCIs exploits sensorimotor rhythms (SMRs), task-related modulations in mu (∼8–13 Hz) and beta (∼13–30 Hz), to decode motor imagery or attempted movement for continuous or discrete control (Pfurtscheller and Neuper, 2001, Wolpaw and McFarland, 2004). Classic pipelines combined band-power features with classifiers (e.g., LDA, SVM) and adaptive training to address user learning and session drift (Blankertz et al., 2008). A major methodological advance came from geometry-aware decoding approaches, which represent EEG signal relationships as points on a mathematical manifold that preserves how channels co-vary across the scalp. By using this representation instead of collapsing signals into arbitrary features, these methods achieve greater robustness to noise and inter-session variability and have become strong baselines for motor-imagery decoding (Congedo et al., 2017).
Clinical translation in EEG BCIs increasingly emphasizes reducing training time, improving long-term reliability, and enabling “everyday” use in rehabilitation and assistive technologies. Shared-control strategies, where EEG decodes high-level intent and autonomous robotics handles low-level execution, have repeatedly proven essential for practical navigation and manipulation tasks, lowering user cognitive load and mitigating decoding errors (del Millán et al., 2010, Pfurtscheller et al., 2010).
Finally, asynchronous (self-paced) operation and error monitoring are key to moving EEG BCIs from cue-based demonstrations to naturalistic interaction. Movement-related cortical potentials (MRCPs) and related low-frequency components can support intention-onset detection without external timing cues, enabling more autonomous control modes(Lew et al., 2012, Mak and Wolpaw, 2009). In parallel, error-related potentials (ErrPs), neural signatures elicited when users perceive a mistake, provide a biologically grounded supervision channel that can be used to veto, correct, or adapt BCI actions online, improving reliability in closed-loop systems (Chavarriaga and Millán, 2010).
2.4. Spinal cord decoding
Electrophysiological recordings from the human spinal cord have historically been developed and deployed primarily for clinical and diagnostic purposes, rather than for real-time neural decoding or brain–computer interface applications. Early and influential work established spinal recordings as tools to assess functional integrity, lesion severity, and prognosis, particularly after SCI. For example, recordings of somatosensory evoked potentials and related signals have been used intraoperatively and post-injury to detect conduction block, monitor ischemia, and predict neurological outcome (Curt and Dietz, 1999, Whittle et al., 1986). In these contexts, spinal signals are interpreted qualitatively or through simple amplitude and latency metrics, rather than decoded as high-dimensional representations of motor or sensory intent.
Reviews of locomotor control emphasize how recordings from spinal interneurons and descending pathways have been used to infer how reticulospinal and propriospinal commands are encoded and transformed into coordinated movement (Deliagina et al., 2002, Kiehn, 2016). However, despite this rich physiological information readout, spinal cord recordings have not yet matured into neural interfaces comparable to more central interfaces. Technical barriers, including mechanical instability, signal attenuation, and limited long-term access, have constrained the development of chronic, high-resolution spinal recording platforms suitable for decoding-based control. Consequently, even recent reviews of human motor decoding focus predominantly on cortical and peripheral nerve signals, with spinal recordings appearing mainly in the context of neuromodulation and clinical monitoring rather than as primary information sources for interfaces (Tam et al., 2019).
2.5. Peripheral nerve decoding
Electroneurographic (ENG) signals offer, in principle, direct access to the highly organized efferent and afferent pathways of the peripheral nervous system (PNS). However, despite decades of research, peripheral nerve recordings are rarely used as stable chronic signal sources, particularly in human applications. Unlike surface EMG or cortical recordings, direct neural recordings from peripheral nerves are highly susceptible to foreign body response, fibrotic encapsulation, axonal degeneration, and electrode migration, which collectively degrade signal quality over time (Micera et al., 2010, Navarro et al., 2005).
Peripheral nerve interfaces span a spectrum of invasiveness with corresponding limitations. Extraneural cuff electrodes provide excellent surgical safety and less invasive, but recorded signals are typically low-amplitude compound action potentials strongly attenuated by the epineurium, resulting in limited SNR and poor selectivity. Intraneural electrodes such as LIFE and TIME arrays offer improved spatial resolution and access to fascicle-level activity, yet they introduce direct trauma to the nerve and reliably provoke inflammatory and fibrotic responses that compromise long-term viability. Consequently, most intraneural interfaces demonstrate recording instability over weeks to months, restricting their use primarily to short-term experiments rather than chronic decoding (Badia et al., 2016). As a result of these constraints, peripheral nerve decoding has not followed the same trajectory as cortical BCIs. Although algorithmic studies demonstrate that motor intent and kinematic variables can be decoded from nerve recordings under controlled conditions, sustained high-performance decoding over long timescales remains elusive. Reviews of human motor decoding consistently emphasize that peripheral nerve signals are far less stable than cortical signals, and that their clinical utility is limited by biological rather than algorithmic factors (Tam et al., 2019).
To circumvent the instability of direct peripheral nerve recordings, the field has increasingly shifted toward hybrid strategies that convert neural activity into more robust bioelectric signals. The most prominent example is the Regenerative Peripheral Nerve Interface (RPNI), in which transected peripheral nerves reinnervate multiple autologous muscle grafts that function as biological amplifiers. This approach effectively leverages the stability and signal amplitude of EMG recordings while confining the implant site to the muscle grafts. Importantly, muscle grafts can support multiple, independently decodable control signals, enabling extraction of multiple degrees of freedom without increasing neural invasiveness (Kubiak et al., 2018, Kung et al., 2014, Vu et al., 2020). A closely related strategy is Targeted Muscle Reinnervation (TMR), in which residual peripheral nerves are surgically redirected to reinnervate alternative muscle targets following amputation. TMR has been widely adopted in clinical practice for prosthetic control and pain management, with studies demonstrating improved signal separability, intuitive motor mapping (Kuiken et al., 2017, Kuiken et al., 2009, Walsh et al., 2023, Zhou et al., 2007).
2.6. Muscle activity decoding
EMG analysis has long occupied a central role in clinical neurophysiology as a tool to characterize muscle activation patterns. In clinical practice, classical approaches such as needle electromyography and motor unit action potential (MUAP) analysis are widely used to assess neuromuscular function and diagnose peripheral and central disorders, providing detailed information on motor unit morphology, recruitment, and pathological changes (Kimura, 2013). However, these techniques are primarily designed for diagnostic evaluation and are less suited for continuous or real-time decoding applications. In contrast, multichannel EMG is increasingly analysed through the lens of muscle synergies, low-dimensional patterns of co-activated muscles that reflect how the nervous system organizes movement (d’Avella et al., 2003). Synergy-based analyses have been widely used to assess motor impairments in stroke, SCI, and other neurological disorders, revealing reduced dimensionality, abnormal merging, or altered recruitment of synergies as signatures of impaired neural control (Cheung et al., 2012, Clark et al., 2010, Tresch et al., 1999).
At the same time, EMG has long been one of the most accessible and intuitive bioelectric signals for motor decoding in humans. Unlike central neural recordings, EMG reflects the final common pathway of motor control: the activation of skeletal muscle fibers by spinal motoneurons. As a result, the EMG signal envelope correlates closely with muscle activation level and force production, enabling relatively direct mappings between recorded activity and intended movement. This favorable physiological relationship has made EMG one of the earliest and most widely used signals for motor decoding in neuroprosthetics and rehabilitation (De Luca et al., 2006, Farina et al., 2014, Scheme and Englehart, 2011).
Surface EMG is particularly attractive due to its non-invasiveness, low cost, and ease of chronic deployment. By placing electrodes over target muscles, surface EMG enables effective direct control strategies, where EMG amplitude or envelope is mapped proportionally to actuator velocity, force, or joint angle. Such mappings align well with natural motor output and require limited user training, contributing to their widespread clinical adoption (Cimolato et al., 2022, Tam et al., 2019). Despite these advantages, surface EMG suffers from well-known limitations. Signals are highly susceptible to cross-talk from adjacent muscles, motion artifacts, changes in skin–electrode impedance, and anatomical variability across subjects and sessions. Muscle fatigue, posture, and electrode displacement further introduce nonstationarity, leading to performance degradation during prolonged or real-world use (Farina et al., 2014, Fougner et al., 2011). From a neurophysiological perspective, surface EMG represents a spatially blurred summation of multiple motor unit action potentials, obscuring the structure of underlying neural commands and limiting decoding specificity.
To mitigate these limitations, EMG decoding has progressively evolved toward multichannel and high-density EMG (HD-EMG) recordings. HD-EMG arrays sample muscle activity over spatial grids, enabling partial separation of motor unit territories and improved estimation of activation patterns. In humans, HD-EMG has enabled decomposition of surface recordings into individual motor unit firing trains under controlled conditions, providing insight into spinal motoneuron output and enhancing decoding potential (Farina and Holobar, 2016, Negro et al., 2016). However, HD-EMG remains highly sensitive to electrode shift, muscle deformation, and limb movement, which limits its robustness during dynamic behaviors and outside laboratory environments (Hug et al., 2021).
A fundamental limitation of EMG decoding arises in neurological conditions where the descending neural connection to muscle is disrupted. In cases of complete SCI, advanced motor neuron disease, or severe peripheral neuropathy, voluntary muscle activation is often absent, rendering standard surface EMG largely unusable for decoding motor intent (Farina et al., 2014). However, emerging evidence suggests that HD-EMG can, in some cases, detect and decode sparse residual motor unit activity even in the absence of overt movement (Tacca et al., 2026). Studies in these individuals indicate that HD-EMG decomposition can reveal voluntarily modulated motor unit firing below the lesion level, demonstrating preserved descending control that is not evident in force production or standard EMG recordings (Miller et al., 2014, Oliveira et al., 2024, Tanzarella et al., 2019). Despite these promising findings, HD-EMG remains highly susceptible to electrode displacement, muscle deformation, and motion artifacts, restricting its applicability in daily-life scenarios and across long time scales. To address these limitations, the field is increasingly moving toward implantable myoelectric interfaces, which provide closer access to motor units and improved chronic stability. Pioneering work using implanted EMG electrodes demonstrates that stable, high-quality myoelectric signals can be recorded over extended periods and support reliable decoding even when surface recordings fail, particularly in individuals with severe motor impairment (Hahne et al., 2016, Jung et al., 2022, Ortiz-Catalan et al., 2014).
3. Encoding technologies: How we “write” into the nervous system
Re-establishing communication between the brain and the body requires not only accurate decoding of neural activity but also the ability to actuate volitional intention through external hardware or physiological interfaces. Over the past decades, a wide range of actuation technologies has emerged, including neurorobotic systems and electrical stimulation systems capable of activating muscles or modulating neural circuits. Although these approaches differ considerably in their mechanisms and intended applications, they share the common objective of restoring functional motor output in individuals with neurological or orthopedic impairments (Fig. 2). Translation of these technologies has recently been shaped by the idea that there is no one-fits-all solution; instead, each system targets a specific combination of impairment severity, residual voluntary function, and therapeutic goals (Coscia et al., 2019, Micera et al., 2020).
