3.1. Robotics and Exosuits
Robotic systems were among the first actuation technologies to reach clinical implementation. These devices were designed to deliver highly repeatable and precisely controlled movements, enabling high-intensity training that exceeds what can typically be provided manually by therapists. The rationale for their introduction into stroke and SCI rehabilitation was initially grounded in the principles of motor learning, which highlight the importance of task specificity, repetition, and early mobilization (Maier et al., 2019).
Robotic gait trainers and upper-limb robots reduce the physical burden on clinicians, allow a higher volume of practice, and improve safety during early rehabilitation sessions, particularly during gait training, which often requires several therapists during conventional therapy. Early clinical studies in stroke demonstrated improvements in motor function and activity performance attributable to robotic assistance (Fasoli et al., 2004, Hsieh et al., 2012). More recently, neuroimaging and neurophysiological studies have shown that robotic training can induce adaptive plasticity in corticospinal and sensorimotor networks (Calabrò et al., 2026), supporting its role not only as an assistive device but also as a neurorehabilitative intervention.
Several exoskeleton systems have progressed from early laboratory prototypes to devices that are now commercially available and cleared for clinical use. Among these, the Hybrid Assistive Limb (HAL) was the first to obtain FDA clearance (FDA protocol number K171909) for bioelectrical signal–based gait rehabilitation in stroke and SCI, marking an important step in the regulatory approval of neuro-controlled exoskeletons. Other systems such as Ekso, ReWalk, Indego, and the Lokomat have since become widely incorporated into rehabilitation practice and are used to support both task-specific gait training and mobility assistance (Tamburella et al., 2022). At the same time, upper limb exoskeletons have been explored, with particular attention to stroke rehabilitation, showing improvements compared to dose-matched traditional therapy, while not meeting the minimum clinically important difference (Park et al., 2025). Across these platforms, clinical studies and meta-analyses have reported improvements in gait speed, endurance, balance, and cardiometabolic parameters in individuals with stroke or SCI, along with reductions in spasticity and enhancements in quality of life (Park et al., 2025, Tamburella et al., 2022). Neurophysiological assessments further suggest that these improvements may be accompanied by adaptive changes in corticospinal excitability and functional connectivity during training (Calabrò et al., 2026). Adverse events remain relatively uncommon and typically mild, supporting the overall safety of exoskeleton-based rehabilitation.
Despite these advances, current exoskeletons still face notable limitations. Clinical adoption is hindered by high device costs: international comparisons indicate substantial heterogeneity in cost-effectiveness across health systems. For example, the United Kingdom has reported high per-session costs due largely to persistent 1:1 therapist–patient ratios even when robotic platforms are utilized (Gower et al., 2025). Many systems remain bulky and difficult to use outside clinical environments, limiting their application as assistive devices for daily life. Furthermore, most clinical studies continue to rely on relatively small samples and short follow-up durations, and many devices have limited adaptability for patients with very severe weakness, contractures, or poor trunk control. Alongside these barriers, heterogeneity in populations, robotic devices and outcome measures limits the accessibility of robotic paradigms in these contexts (Park et al., 2025). These factors collectively limit the generalizability of findings and highlight the need for more scalable and autonomous designs.
Importantly, evidence increasingly suggests that purely passive robotic limb mobilization is insufficient to optimally drive motor recovery. Motor learning and neuroplasticity depend on active engagement of motor intent and sensorimotor integration, whereas passive movement alone elicits limited corticospinal reorganization and weaker functional gains (Lotze et al., 2003, Nudo, 2013).
3.2. Functional electrical stimulation
Functional Electrical Stimulation (FES) is the most commonly used electrical stimulation technique for restoring movement in individuals with neurological motor impairments (Losanno et al., 2023). It has been investigated for more than 60 years as both a rehabilitative and assistive strategy. Unlike external hardware supports such as exoskeletons, FES generates functional movements by directly depolarizing peripheral nerves and activating muscle fibers, enabling movement even in fully paralyzed muscles, provided the neuromuscular junction and muscle fibers are still excitable. Compared to robotic approaches, FES additionally induces peripheral physiological benefits (Sheffler and Chae, 2007), including improvements in muscle mass (Sanna et al., 2025) and enhanced central neuroplasticity (Carson and Buick, 2021, Gandolla et al., 2016). FES’s most established clinical adoption is in stroke rehabilitation, particularly through commercial foot-drop devices (WalkAide by Innovative Neurotronics, L300 by Bioness/Ottobock, ODFS Pace by Odstock). These systems use single-channel stimulation to correct a common gait impairment with low invasiveness, making foot-drop correction the most widespread FES application outside research. Beyond these applications, multi-channel FES has been investigated for more complex motor tasks, especially in the rehabilitation of stroke and SCI, and often in combination with external supports such as orthoses or exoskeletons (Anaya et al., 2018). FES has been applied to a wide range of upper- and lower-limb tasks, including standing, walking, reaching, and grasping (Höhler et al., 2024).
Several FDA-approved or CE-marked systems are now commercially available, enabling standardized and user-friendly deployment of FES in clinical research (e.g., Rehamove and Rehastim by Hasomed GmbH, MyndMove by MyndTec and FES cycling systems RT300 by Restorative Therapies). Although these systems remain costly, they are substantially less expensive than robotic exoskeletons or advanced prostheses.
Nevertheless, despite sustained research interest, FES has not achieved widespread clinical or home-based use for complex motor restoration. Key limiting factors include reduced dexterity and rapid fatigue. Fatigue develops more rapidly during electrically elicited contractions for two main reasons. First, whereas natural fused contractions arise from asynchronous activation of many motor units at low rates (∼ 5 Hz), electrical stimulation activates motor units synchronously and therefore requires higher frequencies (>20 Hz) to achieve fusion. Second, electrical stimulation preferentially recruits large-diameter, fast-fatiguing motor fibers due to their lower electrical thresholds: an “inverse recruitment” order opposite to physiological activation. Comfort and usability also affect clinical translation. Because sensory fibers have lower activation thresholds than motor fibers, FES often activates them concurrently, which may cause discomfort in individuals with preserved sensation. In addition, system setup, usability, and the visibility of external components all influence patient acceptance. Dexterity primarily depends on stimulation selectivity, which differs markedly between non-invasive and invasive approaches.
Surface FES delivers stimulation through adhesive electrodes placed on the skin over the motor points of targeted muscles and connected to an external stimulator (Losanno et al., 2023). Electrodes may be integrated into garments or external supports such as exoskeletons to improve placement consistency. Surface FES is non-invasive, relatively inexpensive, and adaptable to individual patient needs. It has been extensively studied for both upper- (Ambrosini et al., 2021) and lower-limb (Ambrosini et al., 2011, Dell’Eva et al., 2025, Ferrante et al., 2016) rehabilitation in stroke survivors, often combined with robotic or orthotic assistance. Despite this flexibility, surface FES has not gained widespread adoption as a daily assistive technology. Its primary use remains in rehabilitation settings, with commercial systems generally targeting specific, gross motor tasks. Major limitations include low spatial selectivity, restricting movement precision, and the need for individualized electrode placement and stimulation parameter calibration. While such calibration is manageable in clinical environments, it remains challenging for unsupervised home use. To address this limitation, emerging solutions include the use of multi-pad (matrix) electrodes, which eliminates the need for manual electrode placement by enabling “electronic repositioning” through onboard channel switching (Malešević and Antfolk, 2024, Malešević et al., 2012). This flexibility opens the door to more advanced and semi-automated calibration strategies, for example by integrating EMG recordings to identify optimal stimulation sites, such as motor points or nerve junctions (Baumgart et al., 2024, Tacca et al., 2024).
FES electrodes can also be implanted surgically, either intramuscularly or on the epimysium, providing higher selectivity compared with surface stimulation. For this reason, implanted FES has been widely investigated for hand-function restoration. The FreeHand system, for example, was implanted in more than 250 individuals with SCI in the early 2000 s (Peckham et al., 2001), before being discontinued about half a decade later. Similar systems have been evaluated in stroke (Burridge et al., 2011, Spensley, 2007) and SCI (A. Bolu Ajiboye et al., 2017) individuals, though none has achieved broad clinical deployment. The limited adoption of implanted FES is likely related to the surgical complexity, the need for multidisciplinary expertise, and ongoing postoperative maintenance. In recent years, interest has increasingly shifted from muscle or peripheral nerve stimulation toward spinal stimulation approaches. These target the central nervous system more directly, potentially enabling more physiological recruitment order and offering enhanced opportunities for neuroplasticity. Substantial research and industry investment now focus on spinal stimulation as a promising direction for future motor restoration strategies. This shift reflects the ongoing pursuit of stimulation strategies that can achieve high selectivity while maintaining physiological recruitment patterns.
3.3. Peripheral nerve stimulation
Moving more proximally, the next obvious target for neuromodulation are peripheral nerves. PNS differs fundamentally from intramuscular FES in both the site of actuation and the electrode architecture, targeting directly the nerve trunk, where axons of multiple α-motor neurons are bundled together. This allows PNS to activate several functionally related muscles through a single interface (axons of multiple α-motor neurons are bundled together), substantially reducing the number of implanted electrodes and surgical sites required for complex motor restoration (Azevedo Coste et al., 2026, Badi et al., 2021). However, this setup advantage comes at the cost of reduced intrinsic selectivity. Because a single peripheral nerve contains motor fibers projecting to multiple muscles, nerve-level stimulation does not provide the one-to-one muscle targeting achievable with intramuscular electrodes. As a result, selectivity in PNS is not determined by implantation site alone, but depends critically on electrode design, contact configuration, and stimulation strategy (Azevedo-Coste et al., 2019).
To address this limitation, several classes of peripheral nerve electrodes have been developed. Epineural (extraneural) interfaces (FINE and Cuff electrodes), such as nerve cuff electrodes, surround the nerve without penetrating it and are favored for their mechanical stability and long-term safety, but offer limited fascicular selectivity. In contrast, intrafascicular electrodes (LIFE and TIME electrodes) penetrate the nerve to access individual fascicles, providing higher selectivity at the expense of increased invasiveness and potential risks to nerve integrity (Badi et al., 2021). Multi-contact electrodes enable spatial shaping of the electric field, allowing partial discrimination between fascicular groups. Indeed, selectivity can be further modulated through calibration of the stimulation paradigms. In particular, bipolar and multipolar configurations have been shown to reduce current spread and improve spatial confinement of activation compared with monopolar stimulation, leading to more differentiated muscle recruitment within the same nerve (Azevedo Coste et al., 2026). While these strategies do not fully overcome the fundamental limitation that multiple motor pools coexist within a single nerve, they provide a pragmatic balance between selectivity, robustness, and implant complexity. In an effort to further mitigate selectivity constraints while preserving a limited surgical footprint, the ReHAB project employed multi-contact epineural FINE electrodes to stimulate a restricted set of proximal and distal upper-limb nerves under intracortical control (Herring et al., 2024). This approach successfully enabled coordinated reaching and grasping movements of the participant’s own arm; however, motor output remained largely ballistic, with limited capacity for smooth, continuously graded force or velocity modulation. Similar constraints have been reported in the European AI-Hand project, suggesting that epineural nerve-level stimulation, even with advanced decoding and multi-contact interfaces, may be insufficient to fully support fine, continuously modulated motor control (Azevedo Coste et al., 2026). Evidence from primate studies using intrafascicular TIME electrodes suggests that higher-resolution access to individual fascicles can enable more graded and fractionated motor control (Badi et al., 2021). Whether these findings can be translated safely and reliably to human clinical applications remains an open question and represents a key challenge for future peripheral nerve interface development.
3.4. Spinal cord stimulation
SCS has evolved from an analgesic technique into a neuromodulation strategy for motor rehabilitation. Over the past decade, SCS has shown the capacity to amplify residual descending motor commands, engage spinal sensorimotor networks, and restore volitional movement in individuals with severe neurological injury (Courtine et al., 2009). SCS originated in the 1960 s as an application of Melzack and Wall’s gate control theory of pain (1965), using epidural electrodes to modulate dorsal column afferents. Early reports in the 1970s observed reflexive motor responses following epidural stimulation, suggesting activation of spinal circuits beyond analgesia. Modern SCS exploits this principle: electrical currents primarily recruit large‑diameter Ia afferents in the dorsal roots − fibers with the lowest activation threshold due to their diameter and myelination − eliciting a posterior root muscle (PRM) reflex through trans‑synaptic excitation of α‑motoneurons (Minassian et al., 2020). This indirect recruitment preserves physiological muscle synergies and facilitates activity‑dependent plasticity within spared spinal and supraspinal pathways. Depending on the functional target, stimulation is applied over lumbar segments for lower‑limb or cervical segments for upper‑limb motor restoration.
Epidural spinal cord stimulation (eSCS) delivers electrical current into the epidural space via multi-contact paddle leads, with stimulation fields spreading through cerebrospinal fluid to primarily activate dorsal-root afferents and modulate spinal sensorimotor interneuronal networks (Hofstoetter et al., 2018). By increasing the excitability of spinal circuits below the lesion, eSCS enables weak residual supraspinal inputs to regain functional influence over motor output (Balaguer et al., 2025, Greiner et al., 2021). The clinical efficacy of eSCS has been most extensively investigated in SCI. Landmark studies demonstrated that, when combined with intensive task-specific training, eSCS can enable recovery of volitional standing and stepping in individuals with chronic motor-complete SCI (Angeli et al., 2018, Hofstoetter et al., 2015, Moritz et al., 2024, Sasaki et al., 2025, Wagner et al., 2018). Subsequent work refined stimulation strategies by introducing spatiotemporally patterned protocols aligned with gait phases, improving the naturalness and robustness of locomotor control (Rowald et al., 2022). More recently, eSCS has been translated to cervical applications for upper-limb rehabilitation after stroke. Initial human evidence showed that cervical eSCS can acutely facilitate arm and hand movements in individuals with chronic post-stroke hemiparesis (Powell et al., 2023). In 2024, a multi-participant pilot using individualized lead configurations and integrated kinematic feedback confirmed reproducible task-specific facilitation of upper-limb movements and carry-over improvements following training (Capogrosso et al., 2024). Beyond SCI and stroke, exploratory applications of eSCS have been reported across other neurological domains. These include improvements in gait regularity and reductions in freezing episodes in Parkinson’s disease (Milekovic et al., 2023), enhanced muscle strength and walking endurance in spinal muscular atrophy (Prat-Ortega et al., 2025), and mitigation of autonomic dysfunction in chronic cervical SCI (Phillips et al., 2025).
In parallel with epidural approaches, non-invasive SCS methods have emerged as a less invasive alternative for modulating spinal circuits. Transcutaneous spinal cord stimulation (tSCS) applies surface electrodes over the spine to deliver current through the skin and subcutaneous tissue to the dorsal roots. Its mechanism parallels epidural SCS, predominantly recruiting large‑diameter sensory afferents that synaptically excite spinal interneurons and motoneurons, thereby modulating sensorimotor network excitability. Compared with epidural approaches, tSCS is non‑invasive, low‑cost, and easily deployable, but suffers from reduced spatial selectivity and requires higher stimulation amplitudes to achieve comparable neural recruitment.
At lumbar levels, the posterior root muscle reflexes evoked by tSCS and epidural SCS are physiologically similar, confirming common activation of afferent pathways (Hofstoetter et al., 2018). At cervical levels, optimal electrode configurations remain under investigation, with ongoing debate on current paths and muscle selectivity. Similarly to eSCS, tSCS was initially explored as both an assistive and rehabilitative intervention for SCI. In incomplete SCI, it has demonstrated immediate improvements in lower-limb motor output (Gerasimenko et al., 2015, Meyer et al., 2020) and has enhanced gains when combined with task-specific motor training (Hofstoetter et al., 2015, McHugh et al., 2020). Upper-limb applications after cervical SCI have also shown translational potential (Inanici et al., 2021) including evidence from controlled studies reporting improvements in pinch force, hand prehension, and motor scores when paired with rehabilitation (Moritz et al., 2024). Application to the stroke scenario is emerging. Early findings indicate immediate facilitation of lower-limb muscle activation and increased gait velocity after lumbar tSCS in chronic stroke (Lim et al., 2025). Evidence from controlled studies also shows reduced Modified Ashworth Scale scores and improved passive range of motion (Alashram et al., 2023), suggesting that tSCS has benefits in spastic hypertonia modulation, particularly at higher frequencies (≥30 Hz).
Historically, tSCS has been paired with high-frequency carriers to reduce the sensation and pain caused by the stimulation. The issue is still debated, with multiple studies arguing that there is no discomfort reduction when employing carriers (Dalrymple et al., 2023) and that they might be less efficient in generating sensory activation (Keesey et al., 2024).
3.5. Brain stimulation
Brain stimulation technologies constitute a distinct class methods capable of modulating neural activity to generate measurable behavioural effects or provide therapeutic benefits. These approaches range from non-invasive to fully implanted systems and have been extensively investigated across cognitive, neuropsychiatric, and neurological domains. A substantial body of research demonstrates their applicability to cognitive deficits (Wu et al., 2025) and psychiatric disorders (Hyde et al., 2022), particularly in individuals who do not respond adequately to pharmacological interventions (Fitzgerald et al., 2006, Romero et al., 2019). Within the context of motor rehabilitation and restoration, two modalities stand out for their ability to produce clear, quantifiable motor effects: Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation (TMS) and Deep Brain Stimulation (DBS).
Non-invasive brain stimulation (NIBS) techniques, including TMS and transcranial Direct Current Stimulation (tDCS), have been applied to cognitive, linguistic, and motor disorders, as well as to deficits arising from brain lesions such as stroke (Rektorová et al., 2025). Among these approaches, TMS is the best characterized for eliciting direct motor output. TMS delivers brief magnetic pulses that induce electric currents in targeted cortical regions, modulating neuronal excitability according to coil orientation and stimulation intensity. When applied over the primary motor cortex, sufficiently intense pulses generate descending corticospinal volleys that can be recorded in peripheral muscles using surface electromyography (Edwards et al., 2008). Direct actuation of discrete movements is possible; for example, stimulation of the motor cortex can evoke individual finger movements in humans and non-human primates (Romero et al., 2019).
Clinically, TMS has been used both as a diagnostic and therapeutic tool in movement disorders (Edwards et al., 2008). Applications include tremor modulation, where static-field TMS has demonstrated reductions in postural tremor in essential tremor (Urso et al., 2025). Stroke rehabilitation has been the domain of most intensive NIBS development, with upper-limb motor recovery being a primary target in stroke (Rektorová et al., 2025). Early studies suggested potential benefits, but large randomized controlled trials have not consistently confirmed added value during rehabilitation (Harvey et al., 2018). To enhance usability and facilitate translation, recent efforts have explored wearable repetitive TMS devices capable of delivering stimulation with greater portability and reduced setup time (Qi et al., 2025, Tang et al., 2026). tDCS has followed a similar translational trajectory. Preliminary trials in stroke reported improvements in motor outcomes (Bornheim et al., 2020, Garrido et al., 2023), but these findings have not been consistently replicated in larger controlled studies (Cordes et al., 2024). The divergence between early promise and large-scale clinical evidence underscores persistent challenges in dosing, targeting, individual variability, and integration with rehabilitation protocols.
DBS represents the invasive end of the brain stimulation spectrum and is distinguished by its robust and well-characterized motor effects. DBS has long-standing clinical approval for movement disorders, particularly Parkinson’s disease, where stimulation of deep nuclei modulates pathological oscillatory activity and alleviates motor symptoms. Its impact on Parkinsonian symptoms is well established, with multiple controlled studies demonstrating significant and sustained improvements following chronic stimulation (Timmermann et al., 2015, Vitek et al., 2020). DBS has also shown efficacy in the management of chronic pain syndromes (Fasano et al., 2012) expanding its therapeutic scope within motor and sensory domains.
Critically, brain stimulation approaches, should be distinguished from actuation technologies such as FES and robotic systems. While the latter directly generate movement by activating muscles or applying external forces, brain stimulation primarily acts by modulating the excitability and gain of existing neural circuits, enabling residual voluntary commands or task-specific training to become effective. In this sense, these technologies do not create movement de novo, but rather facilitate, gate, or enhance endogenous motor control, aligning them more closely with rehabilitation and plasticity-oriented paradigms than with pure assistive actuation.
4. Closing the loop
At the final stage of the neuroengineering cycle, the central challenge is the integration of motor actuation and sensory feedback into a coherent, functional system. The ultimate goal of closed-loop neurotechnologies is to restore functions in a way that is naturally perceived and intuitively used by the end-users (Markovic et al., 2018). For example, a prosthetic limb should support intuitive voluntary control while simultaneously providing meaningful tactile and proprioceptive information (Raspopovic, 2021). Because neurological injuries rarely affect motor or sensory pathways in isolation, effective solutions must address both domains synergistically (Cimolato and Raspopovic, 2024, Krakauer et al., 2012). Closed-loop sensorimotor neurotechnologies aim to integrate motor intent decoding with sensory feedback delivery to reproduce the bidirectional information flow underlying natural movement. Across implementations, neural or myoelectric signals are decoded to control an external effector, such as a robotic limb, functional electrical stimulation system, or virtual interface, while sensory information from the effector is encoded and returned to the nervous system through central or peripheral stimulation pathways (Daly and Wolpaw, 2008, Jackson and Zimmermann, 2012). Over the past decades, multiple approaches have been proposed to close the sensorimotor loop. The motor decoding, actuation and sensory feedback strategies discussed in the previous sections have been combined and integrated.
4.1. Closed-loop control within the central nervous system
The first compelling demonstration of fully intracortical closed-loop control was provided in non-human primates. In a landmark study, O’Doherty et al. (2011) introduced a brain–machine–brain interface in which a monkey controlled a virtual avatar arm using neuronal recordings from M1, while tactile feedback from the avatar’s hand was delivered via intracortical microstimulation (ICMS) of primary somatosensory cortex (S1). When the virtual hand contacted an object, ICMS pulses conveyed artificial touch sensations that the animal could actively use to guide behavior. Crucially, the animal did not merely react to stimulation but learned to integrate the artificial sensory feedback into motor planning, enabling active sensory exploration (O’Doherty et al., 2011). These concepts were subsequently translated to humans with severe motor impairment (Flesher et al., 2016). In a seminal study, Flesher et al. (2016) implanted one microelectrode array in motor cortex and a second array in S1 of a participant with tetraplegia, creating a fully intracortical bidirectional BCI. Motor cortical activity was decoded to control a robotic arm, while tactile information from sensors embedded in the robotic hand was encoded as ICMS patterns delivered to S1. The addition of artificial tactile feedback produced clear functional benefits. Compared with visual feedback alone, the participant demonstrated more accurate grip-force modulation, improved object manipulation, and faster task completion. Follow-up work further consolidated this concept, establishing bidirectional BCIs as a viable approach for restoring functional sensorimotor interactions (Flesher et al., 2021).
As closed-loop intracortical systems matured, attention shifted toward their long-term stability and real-time robustness. A key challenge is the potential interference between sensory stimulation and motor decoding (Shelchkova et al., 2023). Shelchkova et al. (2023) demonstrated that continuous, high-frequency ICMS in S1 can modulate activity in motor cortex, leading to modest but measurable reductions in decoding performance. Crucially, this interference is not an intrinsic feature of bidirectional architectures, but depends strongly on the stimulation strategy employed. Biomimetic encoding approaches, designed to reflect natural neural population coding through structured, event-related sensory signals, substantially mitigate motor interference while preserving perceptual salience (Bensmaia and Miller, 2014, Callier et al., 2019, Hobbs et al., 2025). These findings underscore that selectivity alone is insufficient for effective sensory feedback. Rather, they reinforce the principle that the functional impact of ICMS depends not only on where information is written in the brain, but critically on how it is encoded. Multiple studies have shown that the perceptual quality and functional utility of ICMS depend on how closely stimulation patterns respect natural population-level coding in S1, rather than on stimulation intensity or spatial precision alone (Tabot et al., 2013, Valle, 2025, Valle et al., 2024).
Advances in electrode technology and stimulation paradigms have further improved the fidelity of intracortical sensory restoration. Multi-electrode stimulation has been shown to evoke more spatially specific and consistent percepts than single-electrode stimulation, supporting finer motor control and more reliable feedback (Greenspon et al., 2024, Hobbs et al., 2025). Beyond immediate performance, early evidence suggests that repeated intracortical sensory stimulation may engage longer-term plastic mechanisms. Preliminary results indicate partial persistence of sensory improvements beyond the stimulation period (Cater et al., 2025), raising the possibility that intracortical BCIs may serve not only as assistive devices but also as tools for sensorimotor rehabilitation.
Intracortical recordings have been used not only in fully intracortical BCIs, but also in hybrid configurations in which cortical decoding is combined with downstream actuation technologies that engage biological or mechanical effectors. One of the most clinically validated examples of this approach couples intracortical decoding with spinal cord stimulation, effectively creating an artificial corticospinal bypass. Work in this framework demonstrated that motor cortical activity can be decoded and translated into spatiotemporally patterned epidural spinal stimulation, enabling volitional control of locomotion and upper-limb movements by dynamically engaging spinal sensorimotor circuit (Bonizzato et al., 2018, Capogrosso et al., 2016, Lorach et al., 2023, Lorach et al., 2022). Intracortical decoding has also been combined with multi-channel FES to directly recruit paralyzed muscles. Motor intent decoded from intracortical recordings was than translated into coordinated muscle stimulations, restoring volitional reaching and grasping in a person with tetraplegia (A Bolu Ajiboye et al., 2017). The same research group further extended this concept to PNS, using intracortical decoding to electrically-driven the motor output from the nerve. This hybrid configuration combined (intracortical motor decoding and stimulation for sensory feedback), while motor actuation was obtained distally, demonstrated for the first time the feasibility of closing the sensorimotor loop by integrating multiple neural interface technologies, even in individuals with complete paralysis (Herring et al., 2024). However, hybrid configurations are not limited to intracortical recordings. Similar architectures have been implemented using ECoG, which provides stable cortical signals suitable for high-level control. ECoG-based systems have demonstrated reliable command-level decoding that can be used to drive robotic assistive devices such as exoskeletons, enabling overground walking and complex whole-body movements in individuals with tetraplegia by translating cortically decoded commands into exoskeleton control signals (Benabid et al., 2019, Larzabal et al., 2021). At a more translational level, EEG-based interfaces have enabled control of wheelchairs and robotic exoskeletons across multiple laboratories in individuals with tetraplegia (Leeb et al., 2007, López-Larraz et al., 2016, del Millán et al., 2010) or upper limb stroke paralysis in stroke survivors (Ang et al., 2014, Colucci et al., 2026). A more focused application for EEG recordings involves their integration with FES, primarily for stroke rehabilitation. Early studies showed that chronic stroke survivors using closed-loop BCI-FES systems achieved significant and lasting upper limb recovery, likely driven by the simultaneous activation of efferent and afferent pathways promoting Hebbian plasticity (Biasiucci et al., 2018). Further long-term studies have confirmed that such systems can sustain neuroplasticity and functional gains over several years, even in elderly patients (Chen et al., 2025). This approach has also been extended to SCI where it was demonstrated that BCI-triggered FES enabled volitional reaching and grasping in individuals with incomplete SCI (Jovanovic et al., 2021).
In parallel with cortical approaches, sensory restoration has also been demonstrated at the level of the spinal cord, particularly in amputee populations. Although these studies have not yet been integrated into fully closed-loop brain–man interfaces, they provide critical evidence that artificial somatosensory feedback can be delivered targeting dorsal column pathways. This approaches have shown to evoke somatotopically organized sensations referred to the phantom limb in both upper and lower amputees, with perceptual qualities suitable for conveying tactile information (Chandrasekaran et al., 2023, Chandrasekaran et al., 2020, Iversen et al., 2024, Nanivadekar et al., 2023).
4.2. Closed-loop control within the peripheral nervous system
Among the most clinically mature approaches for sensory restoration are invasive PNS systems. These involve surgically implanted electrodes that directly interface with peripheral nerves, either wrapped around the nerve (cuff electrodes), pressed onto its surface (flat interface arrays), or inserted within the fascicles themselves (intrafascicular electrodes such as Utah arrays or TIME micro-wires). The value of these interfaces lies in their ability to engage specific populations of sensory fibers, evoking localized and somatotopically organized sensations referred to specific regions of the phantom limb (e.g., fingers, palm, or foot), a finding that has been robustly demonstrated across multiple studies (Graczyk et al., 2018a, Graczyk et al., 2018b, Micera et al., 2010, Raspopovic et al., 2014, Schiefer et al., 2018). These interfaces support functional improvements in object manipulation, grip force control, and locomotion, and their perceptual quality can be further enhanced through biomimetic encoding strategies that better approximate natural afferent firing patterns (Oddo et al., 2016, Petrini et al., 2019a, Valle et al., 2018). However, although these approaches can approximate key features of natural touch and support functional interaction, the elicited sensations remain qualitatively artificial due to the non-selective activation of afferent fibers (Cimolato and Raspopovic, 2024).
Early studies demonstrated that an amputee could reliably discriminate textures such as rough and smooth surfaces through stimulation delivered via a bionic fingertip, which detected surface features and translated them into specific stimulation profiles (Oddo et al., 2016). These sensations were often described as originating from specific locations in the phantom limb, and in some cases resembled pressure or vibration (Graczyk et al., 2018a, Graczyk et al., 2018b, Soghoyan et al., 2023). With continued research in the field, biomimetic encoding, mimicking natural afferent firing via amplitude and frequency modulation, has significantly enhanced the naturalness of sensory feedback, outperforming standard stimulation strategies in both nuance and functional utility (Valle et al., 2018). Longitudinal studies, including take-home trials, have shown that implanted interfaces can remain stable and effective over time, up to after several months of daily use (Ortiz-Catalan et al., 2020). While early work focused primarily on pressure and contact sensations, newer research has begun to incorporate thermal feedback (Iberite et al., 2023). These studies demonstrated that adding temperature cues alongside tactile input enhanced the naturalness and expressiveness of prosthetic interactions, further blurring the line between artificial and biological sensation (Muheim et al., 2024).
Similar techniques are now being extended to the lower limbs. Tibial nerve stimulation, coupled with foot pressure sensors, has allowed transfemoral amputees to regain awareness of ground contact (Petrini et al., 2019a).It was also showed that such feedback not only improved walking confidence and balance but also helped users navigate more complex terrain (Petrini et al., 2019b, Valle et al., 2021).
A growing body of work has investigated non-invasive electrical stimulation as a strategy to restore sensory feedback in prosthetic and neurorehabilitation applications, largely motivated by the desire to avoid surgical risks (Bucciarelli et al., 2023). However, many early non-invasive systems failed to achieve somatotopic correspondence, instead eliciting sensations localized directly under the stimulation electrodes. These approaches therefore relied primarily on sensory substitution rather than true sensory restoration (Jorgovanovic et al., 2014, Schweisfurth et al., 2016, Štrbac et al., 2016). While electrically evoked sensations in these studies improved motor performance and object interaction, users were required to learn abstract mappings of artificial stimulation patterns and sensorimotor variables, with possible impacts in intuitiveness and require training to achieve effective use (Preatoni et al., 2023, Stephens-Fripp et al., 2018). The extent to which different feedback strategies influence embodiment and user preference remains an active area of investigation.
More recent work has demonstrated that, through careful electrode placement over residual functional nerves and appropriate tuning of stimulation parameters, non-invasive stimulation can elicit phantom limb sensations and provide functionally relevant sensory cues at the level of the missing limb. This represents a key conceptual shift toward anatomically meaningful feedback (Stephens-Fripp et al., 2018). In this context, somatotopically matched transcutaneous nerve stimulation has been shown to improve sensorimotor performance (J. Zhang et al., 2024), with subsequent studies demonstrating partial restoration of tactile sensation in amputees supported by objective EEG correlates (Y. Zhang et al., 2024). Complementary biomimetic approaches have further expanded the sensory repertoire, including motion perception (Cimolato et al., 2023, Ding et al., 2025) and event-based encoding strategies for dynamic tactile information such as slip detection and protective reflex feedback (Xie et al., 2024).
Non-invasive sensory feedback has also been explored in lower-limb prostheses and in patients with peripheral neuropathies. Although the evoked sensations are generally less intense and selective than those produced by implanted interfaces, studies in lower-limb amputees have shown that non-invasive feedback can yield comparable cognitive and functional benefits, including improved prosthesis use and reduced attentional demand (Chee et al., 2022a, Chee et al., 2022b, Frumento et al., 2024). Because they can be deployed rapidly and without surgical risk, non-invasive systems are especially suited for use as transitional or standalone interventions (Basla et al., 2022). Importantly, similar sensorimotor deficits are present in peripheral neuropathy, where loss of afferent feedback affects up to 30% of patients. In this population, a pilot study introduced a wearable neuroprosthesis in the form of an electrically stimulating sock that delivered calibrated stimulation to partially intact tibial nerve fibers, resulting in partial restoration of foot sensation, improved gait cadence, and reduced pain after only one day of use (Gozzi et al., 2024a).
Because stable and selective recording of ENG signals remains technically challenging, fully closed-loop brain–body interfaces relying exclusively on peripheral nerve recording and stimulation are still largely unrealized in humans. Consequently, the vast majority of clinically deployed and experimentally validated systems adopt hybrid architectures, combining peripheral nerve stimulation for sensory feedback with motor decoding from muscles or the brain. In upper-limb amputees, this hybrid approach has been successfully implemented using superficial EMG for prosthetic control in combination with invasive electrical stimulation for sensory restoration, in which intraneural electrodes implanted in the median and ulnar nerves delivered patterned stimulation driven by pressure sensors embedded in the prosthetic hand (Raspopovic et al., 2014). Subsequent studies refined this paradigm by distributing stimulation across multiple contacts and employing biomimetic encoding strategies, showing that improvements in the naturalness of sensory percepts translated not only into enhanced sensory perception but also into superior functional performance and prosthesis control (Clemente et al., 2019, George et al., 2019, Valle et al., 2018). In cases of more proximal or severe amputations, TMR has been combined with peripheral nerve stimulation to improve robustness of motor control (Ortiz-Catalan et al., 2020). By rerouting transected nerves to residual muscles, TMR enables reliable decoding of motor intent from reinnervated muscle activity, providing meaningful control signals that can be paired with peripheral nerve stimulation for sensory feedback.
5. Future perspectives: Toward integrated, personalized and biologically grounded neurointerfaces
The next phase of brain–body interfacing will be defined not by a single technological breakthrough, but by the convergence of advances across surgical techniques, bio-interfaces, computational modeling, and adaptive control frameworks. While artificial intelligence and in silico modeling are central enabling tools, their impact will depend on parallel progress in how neural interfaces are physically integrated with the body and how biological signals are accessed, stabilized, and interpreted over long timescales.
Indeed, as brain–body interfaces evolve toward increasingly sophisticated closed-loop architectures, the number of variables that must be considered for effective clinical deployment grows rapidly. Functional outcomes depend not only on the choice of interface technology, but also on patient-specific factors such as lesion level and chronicity, residual sensory pathways, cognitive engagement, fatigue, and ongoing neuroplastic changes. In parallel, device-related parameters, including decoding features, stimulation sites, amplitudes, timing, and encoding strategies, must often be tuned and retuned across sessions and rehabilitation phases. In this context, artificial intelligence (AI) should be viewed not as an autonomous decision-maker, but as a set of computational tools that can assist clinicians in integrating complex data, identifying relevant patterns, and supporting adaptive parameter adjustment of closed-loop systems over the course of rehabilitation. In neurorehabilitation, there is growing advocacy for a paradigm shift toward precision medicine, emphasizing the development of integrative, personalized, and knowledge-driven technologies rather than “one-fits-all” solutions (Micera et al., 2020).
Within this broader ecosystem, AI serves as an integrative layer that connects multimodal data streams, computational models, and control systems. Rather than replacing clinical decision-making, AI enables the extraction of latent structure from high-dimensional neurophysiological data and supports adaptive parameter tuning in complex closed-loop systems. In particular, AI-driven methods are used to extract clinically meaningful features from kinematic, electrophysiological, and multimodal recordings, enabling more precise characterization of motor impairments in conditions such as stroke, Parkinson’s disease, and SCI (Hossain et al., 2023, Mobbs et al., 2024, Rupprechter et al., 2021). Beyond assessment, such approaches have begun to support clinical decision-making in neuromodulation, for example by integrating multimodal neuroimaging and physiological data to inform parameter selection for deep brain stimulation, leading to improved motor outcomes (Roediger et al., 2023). AI is also increasingly applied to longitudinal monitoring, where continuous neurophysiological and behavioural data acquired through wearable systems can be leveraged to track disease progression, infer latent physiological states, and identify biomarkers relevant for therapy adaptation (Aurucci et al., 2023, Gozzi et al., 2024b, Varghese et al., 2024, Yao et al., 2026). Finally, predictive modeling based on neurophysiological and clinical data enables estimation of recovery trajectories and treatment responsiveness, supporting personalized rehabilitation planning and fostering mechanistic insight through explainable models (Gandolfi et al., 2023).
A second major impact of AI is the transition from generic, one-size-fits-all interfaces toward personalized neurotechnologies. Inter-subject variability in neural signals, anatomy, and recovery trajectories makes individualized strategies essential for both decoding and stimulation (Saha and Baumert, 2020). Deep learning enables personalized decoders by capturing user-specific spatial and temporal patterns of activity in EEG and ECoG data (Willett et al., 2021). More recently, large-scale neural network architectures and foundation models trained on extensive neurophysiological datasets have enabled rapid fine-tuning to individual patients, substantially reducing calibration time and user burden (Craik et al., 2019, Lawhern et al., 2018). Importantly, personalization enabled by AI extends beyond decoding to actuation and neuromodulation. Adaptive optimization methods, including reinforcement learning and Bayesian optimization, have been used to adjust stimulation parameters such as pulse frequency, amplitude, and spatial configuration in response to ongoing physiological and behavioral feedback (Aiello et al., 2023, Bonizzato et al., 2023, Borda et al., 2023). These approaches allow stimulation settings to evolve alongside patient physiology, achieving performance comparable to or exceeding expert manual tuning while substantially reducing calibration effort.
Computational modelling has become an essential complementary tool for understanding and optimizing neural interfaces, particularly in the context of electrical stimulation and neuromusculoskeletal coupling. In silico models enable the simulation of neural, musculoskeletal, and biomechanical responses to neural interfaces or robotic systems, thereby supporting device design, prototyping, and optimization prior to or alongside clinical use (Ciotti et al., 2025, Ciotti et al., 2024, Ciotti et al., 2023, Raspopovic et al., 2011). In the context of neurostimulation, such models have been used to represent the electrical activation of targeted peripheral nerves and their interaction with surrounding tissues and inform stimulation strategies (Capogrosso et al., 2013, Cimolato et al., 2023, Ding et al., 2025, Formento et al., 2018, Katic et al., 2023, Valle et al., 2022). Related computational frameworks developed within the neuroengineering community have also integrated neuromusculoskeletal modeling to predict motor output, bridging neural activation with biomechanical function (Durandau et al., 2019, Sartori et al., 2016).
Nevertheless, traditional in silico models are often limited by their standalone nature and by their reliance on fixed parameters, which often fail to capture the substantial inter-subject variability in nerve geometry, tissue conductivity, muscle architecture, and neural recruitment. As a result, models that perform well at the group level frequently show limited predictive accuracy when applied to individual patients. This limitation is particularly critical in neurostimulation, where small differences in electrode placement or tissue properties can lead to large variations in neural activation patterns and functional outcomes (Capogrosso et al., 2013, Raspopovic et al., 2011). To address this challenge, recent efforts have focused on the development of subject-specific models, integrating imaging data (e.g., Magnetic resonance imaging, ultrasound), electrophysiological recordings, and biomechanical measurements to tailor simulations to individual anatomy and physiology. Building on this paradigm, the concept of a digital twin has recently emerged as a promising frontier in computational modeling. However, despite its potential, the creation of fully functional digital twins still lacks validated approaches for real-time personalization and continuous adaptation. Future developments aimed at the integration of longitudinal, patient-specific data streams could enable dynamic updating of model parameters, transforming these models from static analytical tools into adaptive systems capable of both prediction and inference, including the estimation of parameters that are not directly measurable in vivo. In the context of brain–body interfaces, the integration of sensing, modeling, and control within a digital twin framework could enable closed-loop optimization of both decoding and stimulation strategies. This would reduce reliance on manual calibration and support adaptive, patient-specific optimization (Cellupica et al., 2024, Ciotti et al., 2024). For example, a recent study implemented a digital twin of a hand neuroprosthesis within a virtual environment to optimize training strategies for newly amputated individuals, with the model iteratively updated based on user-specific sensor data and performance metrics (Cellupica et al., 2024).
In parallel, complementary efforts are focusing on surgical strategies to establish more reliable and physiologically grounded human–machine interfaces by leveraging residual neuromechanical structures. Innovative approaches such as the Agonist–Antagonist Myoneural Interface (AMI) and the Cutaneous Mechanoneural Interface (CMI) aim to restore more natural sensorimotor dynamics by preserving or reconstructing biomechanical and neural relationships at the amputation site. The AMI reconstructs mechanically coupled agonist–antagonist muscle pairs, enabling proprioceptive feedback via natural muscle stretch receptors and improving both motor control and perceived embodiment (Clites et al., 2018, Srinivasan et al., 2020). In parallel, CMI strategies exploit skin–nerve interactions to provide stable, mechanically mediated sensory feedback, offering an alternative to purely electrical encoding approaches (S. Srinivasan and M. Herr, 2022). These biologically integrated interfaces represent a conceptual shift from purely electronic stimulation toward hybrid bioelectronic systems that exploit residual physiology to generate more naturalistic feedback and long-term stability.
In parallel, advances in regenerative interfaces, including RPNI, CMRPNI, TMR, and TSR continue to expand the interface between biological tissue and artificial systems, improving signal quality and enabling more intuitive control mappings (Kubiak et al., 2021, Kuiken et al., 2017, Shu et al., 2024, Vu et al., 2020). Importantly, these approaches inherently embed subject-specific anatomical and physiological information, further reinforcing the shift toward personalized neurotechnologies.
Future progress will likely be driven by tighter integration between sensing, actuation, and prediction, enabling closed-loop neurotechnologies that continuously adapt to patient-specific neurophysiology. However, translation into routine clinical practice will require addressing several practical challenges. These include standardization of outcome measures across devices and conditions, validation in longitudinal and multi-center studies, and the development of regulatory frameworks that can accommodate adaptive algorithms whose behavior evolves over time. Equally important is the need to preserve clinician oversight and accountability, particularly when algorithmic systems influence stimulation parameters or therapeutic decisions. Transparent models, clear reporting of uncertainty, and clinician-in-the-loop paradigms will be essential to ensure safe and effective deployment.
Ethical considerations become increasingly salient as neurotechnologies move toward long-term use and deeper integration with the nervous system. Issues of data privacy, informed consent, and ownership of neurophysiological data are amplified by continuous monitoring and cloud-based analysis. Moreover, adaptive systems that modify neural activity raise questions about agency, responsibility, and the boundary between rehabilitation and augmentation (Ienca et al., 2025). When developed and deployed responsibly, the integration with closed-loop neurotechnologies has the potential not only to improve functional outcomes, but also to fundamentally enhance how neurological recovery is assessed, guided, and supported within clinical neurophysiology.
